From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent 9992: identify the controller by its AL address

Both agents reject every WSC M2 the controller sends them, and have done so for
their whole uptime. Their own log, 2026-08-01, with LOG_APCFG at trace:

  handle_ap_autoconfig_wsc: Dual controllers detected - rejecting WSC M2 from
  86:f7:56:41:01:d3

That address is the real controller. There is no second one.

agent_update_controller_data() identifies the controller by cmdu->origin. For a
unicast CMDU from a direct neighbour that is the sender's AL address, because
ieee1905d resolves it through the link table. For a relayed multicast it is
not - no link entry matches the source, so origin stays the raw interface MAC.
Measured on both agents, one controller under three origins in eight minutes:

  42x  86:f7:56:41:01:d3   AL address, from unicast AP-Autoconfig Responses
  23x  86:f7:56:41:01:d4   ap-mld-2,   from relayed AP-Autoconfig Search
  23x  7e:04:1c:81:7c:2c   br-lan,     from relayed AP-Autoconfig Search

The br-lan address was latched first and persisted:

  x8, 8g:  mapagent.agent.controller_macaddr = 7e:04:1c:81:7c:2c
  4g:      mapagent.agent.controller_macaddr = 86:f7:56:41:01:d3   (colocated)

so every frame from the controller's other interfaces was "a different
controller while primary is active", multiple_cntlr latched, and the guard at
the top of handle_ap_autoconfig_wsc() returned -1 before touching any state.
The same guard blocks Backhaul STA MLD Config (agent_map.c:6656), which is our
own R6 path.

The consequences are all silent. The radios never reach AUTOCFG_HEARTBEAT, so
agent_reschedule_heartbeat_autocfg() refuses to raise the interval and the mesh
re-runs a full autoconfiguration round every probe_int (20 s) instead of
HEARTBEAT_AUTOCFG_INTERVAL (70 s) - 60% of all 1905 traffic. And the agents keep
serving whatever they were configured with at boot while refusing every change
after it. Nothing else shows it: the census passed for fourteen hours, the
database held 15 of 15 affiliated APs, the mesh forwarded traffic.

The fix is to pass the identity rather than infer it from the frame. The Search
handler already parses and validates the AL MAC Address TLV into `almac` before
any of this, and the TLV is mandatory - verified on the wire, 339 frames of 339
carry it with the correct value. The Response handler has no such TLV and keeps
using origin, which is correct there and is documented as the remaining door.

No manual cleanup is needed. Once the false identity stops being refreshed,
observed_time expires after HEARTBEAT_AUTOCFG_INTERVAL + 10 s and the next
Search re-latches the AL address and rewrites UCI; should_accept_controller()
does not gate on the stored value unless controller_select.local is set, and it
is not.

The debug line now prints the identity and the origin side by side, because the
difference between them is the whole defect and it was invisible.

--- a/src/agent_map.c	2026-08-01 14:02:36.541347847 +0200
+++ b/src/agent_map.c	2026-08-01 14:02:36.541397843 +0200
@@ -1311,20 +1311,52 @@
 	       should_accept_controller(a, origin);
 }
 
-static int agent_update_controller_data(struct agent *a, struct cmdu_buff *cmdu)
+/* Identify the controller by its AL address, which is what identifies a 1905
+ * device - cmdu->origin is whatever the frame was sourced from.
+ *
+ * For a unicast CMDU from a direct neighbour those are the same, because
+ * ieee1905d resolves the sender through its link table. For a relayed
+ * multicast they are not: the sender is several hops or one bridge away, no
+ * link entry matches, and origin stays the raw interface MAC. The agent then
+ * files the controller under an interface address, and the next frame from
+ * ANOTHER interface of that same controller looks like a second controller.
+ *
+ * Measured on hardware 2026-08-01, one controller seen under three origins by
+ * both agents in eight minutes:
+ *
+ *   42x origin 86:f7:56:41:01:d3   <- the AL address, from unicast responses
+ *   23x origin 86:f7:56:41:01:d4   <- ap-mld-2, from relayed Search
+ *   23x origin 7e:04:1c:81:7c:2c   <- br-lan,   from relayed Search
+ *
+ * The br-lan one was latched first and written to UCI, so every WSC M2 from
+ * the real controller was rejected by the multiple_cntlr guard in
+ * handle_ap_autoconfig_wsc() - for fourteen hours, on both agents, while every
+ * other indicator showed a healthy mesh. The agents kept the configuration
+ * they were given at boot and silently refused every change after it.
+ *
+ * The caller passes the best identity it has. AP-Autoconfiguration Search
+ * carries the AL MAC Address TLV and is required to (verified on the wire:
+ * 339 frames of 339), and its handler has already parsed and validated it.
+ * AP-Autoconfiguration Response carries no such TLV, so there origin is all
+ * there is - it is the AL address in practice, and if that ever stops being
+ * true this defect returns through that door.
+ */
+static int agent_update_controller_data(struct agent *a, struct cmdu_buff *cmdu,
+					uint8_t *cntlr_id)
 {
 	int ret = CTRL_MAC_OLD;
 	char mac_str[18] = {0};
 	bool current;
 
-	dbg("%s: cntlr_almac " MACFMT " origin " MACFMT " self " MACFMT "\n", __func__,
-	    MAC2STR(a->cntlr_almac), MAC2STR(cmdu->origin), MAC2STR(a->almac));
+	dbg("%s: cntlr_almac " MACFMT " id " MACFMT " origin " MACFMT " self " MACFMT "\n",
+	    __func__, MAC2STR(a->cntlr_almac), MAC2STR(cntlr_id),
+	    MAC2STR(cmdu->origin), MAC2STR(a->almac));
 
 	a->active_cntlr = true;
 
 	/* Determine if this message is from the current primary controller */
 	current = !hwaddr_is_zero(a->cntlr_almac) &&
-		  !memcmp(a->cntlr_almac, cmdu->origin, 6);
+		  !memcmp(a->cntlr_almac, cntlr_id, 6);
 
 	if (current)
 		timestamp_update(&a->observed_time);
@@ -1338,12 +1370,12 @@
 		/* No active primary - clear dual controller state */
 		a->multiple_cntlr = false;
 
-		if (should_accept_controller(a, cmdu->origin)) {
-			if (!hwaddr_ntoa(cmdu->origin, mac_str))
+		if (should_accept_controller(a, cntlr_id)) {
+			if (!hwaddr_ntoa(cntlr_id, mac_str))
 				return CTRL_MAC_ERROR;
 
-			if (memcmp(a->cntlr_almac, cmdu->origin, 6)) {
-				memcpy(a->cntlr_almac, cmdu->origin, 6);
+			if (memcmp(a->cntlr_almac, cntlr_id, 6)) {
+				memcpy(a->cntlr_almac, cntlr_id, 6);
 				set_value_by_string("mapagent",
 						    "agent",
 						    "controller_macaddr",
@@ -1366,13 +1398,13 @@
 			}
 		} else {
 			/* Message from different controller while primary is active */
-			if (should_disable_local_controller(a, cmdu->origin)) {
+			if (should_disable_local_controller(a, cntlr_id)) {
 				agent_disable_local_cntlr(a);
 			} else {
 				/* Dual controller detected - block configurations */
 				if (!a->multiple_cntlr) {
 					a->multiple_cntlr = true;
-					wifiagent_log_cntlrinfo(a, cmdu->origin);
+					wifiagent_log_cntlrinfo(a, cntlr_id);
 				}
 			}
 		}
@@ -1462,7 +1494,11 @@
 	}
 
 	if (cntlr) {
-		if (agent_update_controller_data(a, rx_cmdu) == CTRL_MAC_NEW) {
+		/* almac comes from the AL MAC Address TLV parsed at the top of
+		 * this function, not from the frame - see the comment on
+		 * agent_update_controller_data().
+		 */
+		if (agent_update_controller_data(a, rx_cmdu, almac) == CTRL_MAC_NEW) {
 			agnt_dbg(LOG_APCFG, "%s: new controller found!"\
 				 " Activate autoconfig configuration"\
 				 " for all radios\n",
@@ -1607,7 +1643,11 @@
 
 	}
 #endif
-	if (agent_update_controller_data(a, rx_cmdu) == CTRL_MAC_NEW) {
+	/* No AL MAC Address TLV in an AP-Autoconfiguration Response; origin is
+	 * the AL address here because the response is a unicast from a direct
+	 * neighbour and ieee1905d resolves those.
+	 */
+	if (agent_update_controller_data(a, rx_cmdu, rx_cmdu->origin) == CTRL_MAC_NEW) {
 		struct wifi_radio_element *r = NULL;
 
 		agnt_dbg(LOG_APCFG, "%s: new controller found! Activate autoconfiguration"\
